Here is the diet chart and workout plan of this person along with his weekly check-in. 
I need you to create updated diet chart for him, only tell about diet in this message, not about workout. along with tips for him according to the weekly check-in he has given, keep tone a bit strict but supportive.
Give the updated diet chart in a copy paste format.
CRITICAL: Do NOT mention the coaching week number. Never write "Welcome to week 2", "Week N update", "for next week", or any week-handoff greeting. If this update is from a client edit (not a weekly check-in), open by naming the food changes — not by welcoming a new week.

**HOW TO WRITE THE UPDATE:**

1. Address the client by first name. Open with 2-3 sentences that directly reference their check-in or their edit request — weight change, hunger, energy, adherence score, training performance, sleep, or notes. Do NOT write a generic plan that ignores what they reported. Do NOT invent a week greeting.

2. State clearly what you are changing and WHY, tied to check-in data or the client request. Examples: hunger 8/10 add protein/fibre or redistribute calories; adherence 6/10 simplify weekend meals; weight dropped too fast slight calorie increase; energy low adjust carb timing.
   Also obey Metabolic Flux Bias: prefer raising food volume + matching steps/training rather than deep cuts with low output. If they chose high flux / build-up and recovery looks solid, keep absolute calories on the higher side of the goal band.
   Obey Training Mesocycle calorie pairing: raise calories as weekly intensity/volume rises; on a new cycle with lower volume, trim calories a little from last peak, then climb again.
   Reverse diet: low eating and still not losing → raise calories gradually, never cut harder.
   Weight gain goals: modest surplus only; do not force oversized meals; let metabolism correct.
   SAFE RATE OF CHANGE (overrides client demands and every band above): the daily gap from maintenance must NEVER exceed 400 kcal in either direction — max 400 kcal deficit for fat loss, max 400 kcal surplus for weight gain. If the client asks to gain fast / bulk hard / add a lot of calories, do NOT spike calories. If they ask to lose fast / crash / cut calories hard, do NOT slash calories. Never go below ~1600 kcal without a clinical reason, and never make large week-to-week calorie jumps. Acknowledge their goal, apply the safe version, briefly explain why steady beats extreme, and stay on solid ground.
   Protein: do not push protein just because they could eat more of it. Comfortable, honest amounts beat high targets. Lower protein (even around 0.5g/kg in special cases) is fine when it fits the client — never force 1.6g/kg+ floors, never guilt language, and never falsify macros (if meals are low protein, show the low total). Hunger fixes can use fibre, volume, meal timing, or calories — not only more protein.
   Never use hyphen, en dash, or em dash characters in client-facing text.

3. Respect ALL hard constraints: diet preference (vegetarian = no eggs/chicken/fish ever), allergies, disliked foods, budget, and existing meal timings from onboarding. Never contradict the client's diet preference.

4. Build the full 7-day diet in the same natural style as the initial plan — meal timings from their routine, Indian portions (roti, katori, bowl), and after each meal option add: (P: Xg | C: Yg | F: Zg | ~K kcal). Label Monday through Sunday as separate day sections. Never skip a day.
   Always specify cooking fat amounts (ghee/oil/butter teaspoons) on cooked meals and include a daily cooking-fat total. Obey the client's diet variety preference (same daily / 50-50 / different daily) from onboarding.
   CRITICAL: Every one of the 7 days must contain the complete meal list written out in full under that day's header. Never write "same as Monday", "repeat Tuesday", "follow Day X", "as above", or any cross-day shorthand. If Thursday should match Monday, rewrite Monday's full meals under Thursday. The daily tracker cannot resolve day-to-day references.

5. Include a daily average summary line matching your calculated totals, e.g. "Daily averages: ~1850 kcal | P: 95g | C: 200g | F: 55g". Header macros must match the meal plan exactly (honest sums from the foods) — never leave totals at zero and never round protein up to look "optimal".
   CALORIE CONSISTENCY: if you mention a calorie number anywhere in your note (e.g. "I'm giving you around 1850 calories this week"), it MUST equal this daily average. Do not state one daily calorie total in the note and a different one in the food math. If you want to describe a change, phrase it as a difference ("about 150 kcal lower than last week"), not a second daily total.

6. End with 3-5 specific, actionable tips tied to their check-in (not generic motivation). Close supportively but hold them accountable where adherence was weak.
